/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Two species need one number, DAB.
   - An n-component mixture has n species with mole fractions y₁, y₂, …, yn that add to 1.
   - Treat the mixture with a single effective diffusivity only when one species is dilute, or when the rest move as one block.
   - Stefan and Maxwell wrote the balance as driving force against friction.
   - yi and yj are mole fractions; Ni and Nj are molar fluxes relative to a fixed frame, mol/(m²·s); c is the total molar concentration, mol/m³; Dij is the binary diffusivity of the pair i–j, m²/s; z is distance, m.
   - Read the right side as friction.
   - Check the two-species case.
   - the first term is the diffusion part and the second is the bulk-flow part carried by the mixture, in mol/(m²·s).
   - When the two fluxes are equal and opposite, N₁ + N₂ = 0 and plain Fick's law remains.
   - Solving n coupled equations by hand is slow.
*/
