/* book exam traps: Averaging diffusivities as yB DAB + yC DAC. Wilke's formula averages resistances, yj/Dij, not D itself. The two answers differ in size and in trend. | Using Ni = −c Di,m dyi/dz and forgetting the bulk term yi ΣN. That is valid only when ΣN = 0; for diffusion through a stagnant mixture it under-predicts the flux by the factor (1 − yi): 10 % at yi = 0.1, half at yi = 0.5. | Carrying one Di,m from the bottom of a column to the top. It is a local value tied to the local composition, so the flux equation has to be integrated. | Putting all n² diffusivities in a table. Only n(n−1)/2 are distinct, and Dij = Dji. Examiners like this count. | Mixing frames: the Stefan–Maxwell equations use Ni, the flux relative to a fixed frame, not Ji, the diffusion flux relative to the molar average velocity. The two differ by yi ΣN. */
