/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Fick's second law gives concentration as a function of position and time.
   - c is concentration of the diffusing species in mol/m³; t is time in s; x is position in m; D is diffusivity in m²/s.
   - To solve it, fix the starting state and the surface state.
   - Fo is the mass Fourier number (dimensionless); D is diffusivity in m²/s; t is time in s; L is half-thickness of a slab or radius of a cylinder or sphere in m; Bim is the mass Biot number; kc is the external mass transfer coefficient in m/s.
   - Fo measures how far diffusion has progressed.
   - At very short contact times, or in a thick body, the far side feels nothing.
   - c is concentration at distance z and time t; c₀ is initial uniform concentration; cs is surface concentration held constant; z is distance from surface in m; D is diffusivity in m²/s; erfc is the complementary error function.
   - This is the penetration idea: only a layer of order √(Dt) changes.
   - C is centre concentration at time t; C₀ is initial uniform concentration; Fo = D t/L² is the mass Fourier number; L is half-thickness in m.
   - Use the first-term centre result when Fo > 0.2; there the neglected terms change C/C₀ by less than 1 %.
*/
